    65	## Product thesis shift
    66	
    67	A plain left rail is useful, but it is no longer enough to differentiate Ocean.
    68	If desktop begins and ends with "sidebar + center chat + right tools," Ocean
    69	risks becoming another competent agent shell that still feels formulaic.
    70	
    71	The more distinctive desktop-native move is:
    72	
    73	> **Ocean uses a Dynamic Island as the live surface for active work, and `⌘P`
    74	> turns that Island into the keyboard-first focus/search surface for sessions,
    75	> memory, and background jobs.**
    76	
    77	That means:
    78	
    79	- **Island = attention router**
    80	- **Center = focused transcript**
    81	- **Workbench = tools and authoring**
    82	- **Rail / palette / modal = utility browse surfaces, not the signature move**
    83	
    84	## Information architecture
    85	
    86	On Tauri desktop, Ocean should resolve into three primary zones plus two command
    87	layers:
    88	
    89	```text
    90	┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    91	│ header/titlebar: brand · Dynamic Island · daemon/runtime · ⋯      │
    92	├───────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┤
    93	│ FOCUSED TRANSCRIPT / ACTIVE WORK      │ RIGHT WORKBENCH            │
    94	│ turns · streaming · permissions       │ Files · Editor             │
    95	│ artifacts · composer                  │ Browser · Repo · GitHub    │
    96	└───────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┘
    97	           ⌘P = focus/search via the Island   ·   ⌘K = actions
    98	```
    99	
   100	A browse surface (rail, modal, or richer palette results) still exists, but it
   101	is not the hero. The hero is the titlebar-adjacent Island that represents work
   102	in motion.
   103	
   104	### The Dynamic Island (desktop-native, ambient, alive)
   105	
   106	The Island is a compact, persistent surface in the header/titlebar zone.
   107	
   108	Its job is to show **active work that may need attention**:
   109	
   110	- active sessions
   111	- sessions waiting on a user reply
   112	- permission / approval requests
   113	- browser-driving state
   114	- call / room state
   115	- CI / GitHub runs later
   116	- longhouse / council work later
   117	
   118	The Island is not just a status chip. It is the desktop-native **attention
   119	router**.
   120	
   121	#### Island behavior
   122	
   123	- Quiet when nothing is urgent.
   124	- Glows / pulses when a session or job needs attention.
   125	- Expands on hover/click into an overlay stack.
   126	- Lets the operator switch the focused session in one move.
   127	- The previously focused session slots back into the Island as background work.
   128	- Later waves can add quick reply / quick approve / quick open affordances.
   129	
   130	#### What the compact Island shows first
   131	
   132	V1 should keep the model small and legible:
   133	
   134	- the currently focused session
   135	- one or more active-background sessions
   136	- a simple state badge (`active`, `needs reply`, `approval`, `running`)
   137	- a compact count for additional active items
   138	
   139	### The Focused Center (transcript-native)
   140	
   180	### Browse surfaces (utility, not hero)
   181	
   182	Ocean still needs a way to browse older or inactive work. That can be supplied
   183	by one or more utility surfaces:
   184	
   185	- existing Sessions modal
   186	- a future slim rail
   187	- richer grouped results inside `⌘P`
   188	- project/session browsing via the palette
   189	
   190	The important rule is that **browsing is not the signature interaction**.
   191	The signature interaction is the Island + focus switching.
   192	
   193	### Command layers
   194	
   195	Ocean should separate **focus/search** from **actions**.
   196	
   197	#### `⌘P` — focus and search
   198	
   199	`⌘P` opens the Island in keyboard-first search mode.
   200	
   201	Its job is to answer:
   202	
   203	- what session do I need?
   204	- what active work needs me?
   205	- where did we talk about this?
   206	- what file / approval / CI run / browser task should I jump to?
   207	
   208	`⌘P` is therefore **not** a second generic command palette. It is Ocean's
   209	focus/search surface.
   210	
   345	## Implementation slices (current recommendation)
   346	
   347	### Slice 1 — Dynamic Island shell
   348	
   349	This is the immediate priority.
   350	
   351	Goals:
   352	
   353	- mount a compact Island in the Tauri header/titlebar zone
   354	- show the focused session and active-background sessions
   355	- reflect simple live states (`active`, `needs reply`, `approval`, `running`)
   356	- expand into an overlay on hover/click
   357	- keep the browser/web model intact
   358	
   359	Success condition: Ocean stops reading as a web chat in a native window and
   360	starts reading as a native workspace with a living attention surface.
   361	
   362	### Slice 1.5 — Focus switching
   363	
   364	Goals:
   365	
   366	- selecting an Island item promotes that session into the center transcript
   367	- the previously focused session slots back into the Island
   368	- preserve room/call/browser and workbench behavior
   369	
   370	Success condition: one session is foreground and the rest orbit naturally.
   371	
   372	### Slice 2 — `⌘P` search mode
   373	
   374	Goals:
   375	
   376	- open the Island in keyboard-first search mode
   377	- search session metadata: title, project, path, branch, origin, state
   378	- group and rank focus results
   379	- Enter focuses, arrow keys navigate, Esc collapses
   380	
   381	Success condition: `⌘P` becomes Ocean's native quick-focus / quick-open surface.
   382	

